cargo fmt --check is red repo-wide on master: 77 diffs across five files, so any lane that runs cargo fmt makes its own diff unreviewable


## Flagged by lane-enum during B120, 2026-08-31

`cargo fmt --check` is red **repo-wide on master**: 77 diffs across
`src/cli/mod.rs`, `src/server/completion.rs`, `interface_impl.rs`,
`relocate_symbol.rs`, and ~40 pre-existing spots in `src/typecheck/checker.rs`.

It is **not a gate**, so nothing is failing. The problem is the trap it sets.

## Why this is a ticket and not a one-line fix

**Anyone who "just runs `cargo fmt`" inside a lane makes their own diff
unreviewable** — 77 unrelated hunks land in a lane commit alongside the three
files the lane actually touched, and the reviewer cannot separate them. That is
the same shape as the `git add -A` and `reset --soft master` hazards already in
`docs/verification-hazards.md`: one command that sweeps in work the author did
not do, produces a commit that applies cleanly, and defeats review rather than
any test.

lane-enum did the right thing — hand-fixed the single nit that was its own,
verified its new module was fmt-clean, and left the other 77 alone.

## The fix, and the ordering constraint that makes it awkward

Run `cargo fmt` on master as **one commit that does nothing else**, then
optionally add `--check` to a gate so it cannot drift again.

**Do it when no lane is in flight.** A repo-wide reformat is a merge-conflict
generator: every lane branch touching those five files gets a conflict in
territory neither side meaningfully edited. Coordinate, or the cure costs more
than the disease. `src/typecheck/checker.rs` is the most-contended file in this
batch and carries 40-odd of the 77.

Add the resulting commit SHA to `.git-blame-ignore-revs` so `git blame` still
works through it.

## Decide deliberately, do not assume

Check whether the repo has a `rustfmt.toml` and whether the 77 diffs are
default-rustfmt opinions or a config the project deliberately does not follow.
"The formatter disagrees" and "the code is misformatted" are different findings,
and only the second justifies rewriting five files.


## HOLD CONDITION — set by Max, 2026-09-01

**Do not run `cargo fmt` until ALL work in flight is merged, including the other
session's.** Not just this session's lanes.

At the time of writing that means:

| holder | tickets | status |
|---|---|---|
| lane-unused | B119 | in flight |
| lane-enum | B121 | in flight |
| lane-resolver | B123 | in flight |
| **peer session `claude-xenon-side-u7o7`** | **B113, B114** | **in flight** |

**B114 is the one that makes this non-negotiable.** It is a pooling change with
a consumer ripple that reaches `checker.rs`, `hover.rs`, `completion.rs` and
`code_actions/`, and it is being reworked pre-merge to thread field and property
visibility. `src/typecheck/checker.rs` carries roughly 40 of the 77 fmt diffs
and is the most-contended file in the batch. A reformat landing while B114 is
open produces a conflict in every one of those files, in territory neither side
meaningfully edited, and the person resolving it cannot tell a real edit from a
whitespace one.

The peer session has been asked to tell us when its lanes are fully merged.
**Both conditions must hold — this session's lanes drained AND the peer's — and
they must hold at the same time.** Two sessions draining at different moments is
not a window; re-check both immediately before running it.

## Why this ordering is the whole ticket

The formatting itself is trivial. The ONLY difficulty is scheduling, and getting
it wrong costs other people's review time rather than producing a test failure.
That is the same shape as `git add -A` and `reset --soft master` in
`docs/verification-hazards.md`: a clean-applying commit that sweeps in work the
author did not do and defeats review rather than any gate.